  "body": "<p>Mirra Andreeva's FAGE WTA Finals debut was a winning one Monday at Indian Wells. The 19-year-old won the final seven games to defeat Elina Svitolina 6-4, 6-0 in 70 minutes.</p>\n<p><span data-teams=\"true\"><a id=\"menur15oa\" class=\"fui-Link ___1q1shib f2hkw1w f3rmtva f1ewtqcl fyind8e f1k6fduh f1w7gpdv fk6fouc fjoy568 figsok6 f1s184ao f1mk8lai fnbmjn9 f1o700av f13mvf36 f1cmlufx f9n3di6 f1ids18y f1tx3yz7 f1deo86v f1eh06m1 f1iescvh fhgqx19 f1olyrje f1p93eir f1nev41a f1h8hb77 f1lqvz6u f10aw75t fsle3fq f17ae5zn\" title=\"https://www.wtatennis.com/where-to-watch-tennis\" href=\"https://www.wtatennis.com/where-to-watch-tennis\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Link Find out where to watch FAGE WTA Finals at Indian Wells\"><strong>Find out where to watch the FAGE WTA Finals at Indian Wells</strong></a></span></p>\n<p><span data-teams=\"true\">The victory improved Andreeva to 1-0 in Group 2 play, joining Aryna Sabalenka atop the standings. It also improved her record against Svitolina to 2-1 and avenged her loss at this year's Australian Open.</span></p>\n<p><span data-teams=\"true\">One of the match's main storylines was the experience gap between Andreeva, making her WTA Finals debut, and Svitolina, the 2018 champion. They entered as the players with the largest age gap (12 years, 229 days) to face one another at the year-end championships since Venus Williams and Caroline Garcia in 2017.</span></p><div class=\"articleWidget center\">\n</div><p><span data-teams=\"true\">In the end, youth prevailed as Andreeva became the fourth higher seed to win in the first four matches of this year's singles event, joining Sabalenka (vs. Karolina Muchova), Linda Noskova (vs. Marta Kostyuk) and Coco Gauff (vs. Jessica Pegula).</span></p>\n<p><span data-teams=\"true\">Here's more on Andreeva's win by the numbers:</span></p>\n<p><strong><span data-teams=\"true\">4: </span></strong><span data-teams=\"true\">It's the fourth time since the format's reintroduction in 2003 that all four higher seeds have won their opening round-robin matches, joining 2009, 2012 and 2024. Potential good news for No. 3 seed Andreeva? In both 2012 (Serena Williams) and 2024 (Gauff), the No. 3 seed went on to win the title.</span></p><div class=\"articleWidget center\">\n<div class=\"livelike-embed widget theme-purple has-bg page-grid\">\n    <section\n        class=\"livelike-embed__content\"\n        data-widget=\"livelike/render-livelike-embed\"\n        data-widget-id=\"bd08957e-dc34-4644-a8c8-c3d7d85683c2\"\n        data-widget-type=\"Text Quiz\"\n    ></section>\n</div>\n</div><p><span data-teams=\"true\"><strong>7:</strong> Andreeva held serve throughout a match for the seventh time this season. She saved all six of the break points she faced against Svitolina, including four in the final game.</span></p>\n<p><span data-teams=\"true\"><strong>51:</strong> Only Elena Rybakina (54) has claimed more WTA Tour Driven by Mercedes-Benz wins this season than Andreeva (51).</span></p>\n<p><span data-teams=\"true\"><strong>87:</strong> Andreeva won 87% of her first-serve points in the opening set, ensuring the break she secured on a Svitolina double fault for 3-2 was all she needed to claim the opener.</span></p><div class=\"articleWidget center\">\n\n\n</div><p><strong><span data-teams=\"true\">603: </span></strong><span data-teams=\"true\">Andreeva defeated Sabalenka in their most recent meeting 603 days ago in the 2025 Indian Wells final. The title made her the third-youngest champion in tournament history behind Martina Hingis (1998) and Serena Williams (1999).</span></p>\n<p><span data-teams=\"true\">With that said, Sabalenka owns a 4-2 lead in the head-to-head.</span></p>",
  "author": "WTA Staff",
